## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design runtime-extensible pipelines for cross-cutting concerns without touching business logic. It probes understanding of the Decorator/Chain patterns, module registries, and dynamic composition under real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Decorator/Middleware pattern for composition
- Runtime registry for plugins by name
- Per-route configuration and execution order
- Async compatibility and fault isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal TypeScript-like skeleton
interface Context { /* request-scoped data */ }
interface Response { /* response data */ }

interface Concern {
  handle(context: Context, next: () => Promise<Response>): Promise<Response>;
}

interface ConcernFactory {
  create(): Concern;
}

class Registry {
  private concerns = new Map<string, ConcernFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: ConcernFactory): void {
    this.concerns.set(name, factory);
  }

  get(name: string): ConcernFactory | undefined {
    return this.concerns.get(name);
  }
}

class Pipeline {
  constructor(private registry: Registry) {}

  build(
    concernNames: string[],
    coreHandler: (context: Context) => Promise<Response>
  ): (context: Context) => Promise<Response> {
    const concerns = concernNames
      .map(name => this.registry.get(name)?.create())
      .filter(Boolean);

    return concerns.reduceRight(
      (next, concern) => (context) => concern.handle(context, next),
      coreHandler
    );
  }
}
```

## How It Works

1. **Registration**: Concerns register factories by name in the Registry
2. **Configuration**: Routes specify concern names in configuration
3. **Resolution**: Pipeline looks up each name and creates Concern instances
4. **Composition**: Chain builds by wrapping concerns around core handler
5. **Execution**: Each concern can modify context, call next(), or short-circuit

This design enables runtime extensibility while maintaining clean separation between cross-cutting concerns and business logic.

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a runtime-extensible command processing system while balancing dynamic plugin loading with reliability and isolation. It surfaces trade-offs between discoverability, versioning, and failure handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Command pattern** with a pluggable registry for runtime flexibility
- **Runtime registration** of handlers enabling plugin-based architecture
- **Idempotency guarantees** to prevent duplicate processing
- **Isolated execution** to ensure fault containment between commands
- **Retry policy** with exponential backoff for transient failures
- **Testing implications** for dynamic behavior and handler contracts

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Command {
  name: string;
  payload: unknown;
  idempotencyKey?: string;
}

interface CommandHandler<T = unknown> {
  handles: string;
  execute(payload: T): Promise<void>;
}

class CommandDispatcher {
  private handlers = new Map<string, CommandHandler>();

  register(handler: CommandHandler): void {
    this.handlers.set(handler.handles, handler);
  }

  async dispatch(command: Command): Promise<void> {
    const handler = this.handlers.get(command.name);
    if (!handler) throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${command.name}`);
    await handler.execute(command.payload);
  }
}
```

## Trade-offs

| Concern | Trade-off |
|---------|-----------|
| Runtime flexibility | Adds complexity; handlers must implement strict contracts |
| Isolation | Worker processes increase memory overhead |
| Idempotency | Requires persistent storage; may limit throughput |
| Discovery | Central registry can become a bottleneck at scale |

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a pluggable CLI core that remains simple yet extensible, a common pattern in large frontend and backend tools at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Plugin architecture
- Runtime dynamic imports
- Registry pattern
- Aliases and help output
- Robust error isolation for plugins

## Code Example

```typescript
export interface PluginCommand {
  name: string;
  description: string;
  execute(args: string[]): Promise<number>;
}

export class CommandRegistry {
  private commands = new Map<string, PluginCommand>();
  private aliases = new Map<string, string>();

  async load(name: string, path: string): Promise<void> {
    const mod = await import(path);
    const cmd: PluginCommand = mod.default ?? mod;
    this.commands.set(name, cmd);
  }

  addAlias(alias: string, name: string) {
    this.aliases.set(alias, name);
  }

  async execute(name: string, args: string[]): Promise<number> {
    const actual = this.aliases.get(name) ?? name;
    const cmd = this.commands.get(actual);
    if (!cmd) throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${name}`);
    try {
      return await cmd.execute(args);
    } catch (e) {
      console.error(`Command failed: ${name}`, e);
      return 1;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you sandbox plugins to prevent memory leaks or long-running tasks?
- How would you version and rollback plugin changes in a live CLI?
- How would you test command loading and error handling end-to-end?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates building a pluggable scheduling system where policies evolve without changing the core orchestrator. Focus on isolation, runtime extensibility, and safe concurrent access.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for interchangeable scheduling algorithms
- Registry pattern for runtime policy registration
- Thread-safe, lazy-loaded registry and policy implementations
- Handling of job metadata for policy selection

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Job {
  id: string
  priority: number
  deadline?: Date
  resources?: any
}

interface SchedulingPolicy {
  pickNext(jobs: Job[]): Job | null;
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private policies = new Map<string, SchedulingPolicy>();

  register(name: string, policy: SchedulingPolicy): void {
    this.policies.set(name, policy);
  }

  get(name: string): SchedulingPolicy | undefined {
    return this.policies.get(name);
  }
}

// FIFO Policy
class FIFOPolicy implements SchedulingPolicy {
  pickNext(jobs: Job[]): Job | null {
    return jobs.length > 0 ? jobs[0] : null;
  }
}

// Priority Policy
class PriorityPolicy implements SchedulingPolicy {
  pickNext(jobs: Job[]): Job | null {
    return jobs.sort((a, b) => b.priority - a.priority)[0] || null;
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PolicyRegistry();
registry.register('fifo', new FIFOPolicy());
registry.register('priority', new PriorityPolicy());

const policy = registry.get('priority');
const nextJob = policy?.pickNext(jobQueue);
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design pluggable runtime components with hot-swapping capabilities, focusing on minimal interfaces, a registry, and safe switching in production.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern
- Registry/Plugin architecture
- Concurrency-safe state management
- Observability and metrics
- Hot-reload without downtime

## Code Example

```typescript
export interface ThrottlingStrategy {
  tryAcquire(tokens: number, ctx?: any): boolean;
  name: string;
}

export class Registry {
  private factories = new Map<string, () => ThrottlingStrategy>();
  register(name: string, factory: () => ThrottlingStrategy) { this.factories.set(name, factory); }
  create(name: string): ThrottlingStrategy { const f = this.factories.get(name); return f ? f() : new (class Default implements ThrottlingStrategy { name = 'default'; tryAcquire() { return true; } })(); }
}

export class ThrottlingManager {
  private strategy?: ThrottlingStrategy;
  constructor(private registry: Registry) {}
  configure(name: string) { this.strategy = this.registry.create(name); }
  permit(tokens: number, ctx?: any): boolean { return this.strategy?.tryAcquire(tokens, ctx) ?? true; }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Runtime extensibility with a registry-based plugin system tests practical design patterns for API gateways: dynamic composition, isolation, and observability under load.

## Key Concepts
- Registry pattern for dynamic route registration
- Chain of Responsibility / Pipeline as the execution model
- Per-route isolation via isolated Context objects
- Parallel execution for independent processors using Promise.all
- Robust error handling and observability hooks

## Code Example
```typescript
type Context = { routeKey: string; req: any; res: any; data?: any };
interface Processor { handle(ctx: Context, next: () => Promise<void>): Promise<void>; }
interface RouteConfig { path: string; method: string; pre: Processor[]; handlers: Processor[]; post: Processor[]; }
class Registry { private routes = new Map<string, RouteConfig>(); register(key: string, cfg: RouteConfig) { this.routes.set(key, cfg); } get(key: string) { return this.routes.get(key); } }
async function runPipeline(ctx: Context, cfg: RouteConfig) {
  for (const p of cfg.pre) await p.handle(ctx, () => Promise.resolve());
  for (const h of cfg.handlers) await h.handle(ctx, () => Promise.resolve());
  for (const po of cfg.post) await po.handle(ctx, () => Promise.resolve());
  return ctx;
}
```

